{
  "understandable": {
    "score": 4,
    "rationale": "The test mixes domain concepts (“gate”, “thresholds”) with low‑level JSON‑L scaffolding, making the intent opaque to a newcomer.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Add a high‑level docstring or comment describing the business rule in plain language.",
      "Extract the payload into a named fixture or helper function with a descriptive name.",
      "Replace the generic assert with one that includes an explanatory message."
    ]
  },
  "maintainable": {
    "score": 3,
    "rationale": "It hard‑codes internal configuration values and the exact shape of the input payload, so any refactor of `BGateConfig` or `compute_b_metrics` will likely break the test.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Drive thresholds from production defaults (e.g., `BGateConfig.default()`).",
      "Use domain‑level builder helpers instead of raw dict literals.",
      "Test the public API/Gate object rather than internal metric functions."
    ]
  },
  "repeatable": {
    "score": 9,
    "rationale": "All external resources are confined to a temporary directory; the data set is deterministic and no network/DB is involved.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Use the public serialization API instead of the private `_write_jsonl` helper to avoid flakiness."
    ]
  },
  "atomic": {
    "score": 5,
    "rationale": "The assertion checks only `metrics[\"pass\"]` while the underlying function produces many metrics; a failure gives very little clue about the root cause.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Assert each relevant metric individually with clear messages.",
      "Separate edge‑case tests for each threshold to keep the high‑level pass test atomic."
    ]
  },
  "necessary": {
    "score": 4,
    "rationale": "The test does not demonstrate a unique scenario that cannot be covered by more granular metric‑level tests; it appears to be a “happy‑path” sanity check.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Parameterise the test to cover boundary values that are not exercised elsewhere.",
      "Consolidate similar tests into a data‑driven suite to reduce duplication."
    ]
  },
  "granular": {
    "score": 4,
    "rationale": "It verifies a high‑level boolean without asserting any of the intermediate values (e.g., `unsupported_rate`, `anchor_match_rate`) that the gate actually evaluates.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Expose metric values and assert them against expected numbers.",
      "Split the test into separate cases for each individual gate rule.",
      "Define named constants for expected rates to improve readability."
    ]
  },
  "fast": {
    "score": 9,
    "rationale": "No sleeps, no heavy I/O; the whole execution finishes in a few milliseconds."
  },
  "first_tdd": {
    "score": 3,
    "rationale": "The test is written in a “full‑stack” style (arrange → write file → call production code → single assert) rather than a minimal, specification‑first unit test that drives the design.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Start with a minimal behavior‑first test that only constructs the essential sample and asserts acceptance.",
      "Avoid writing to disk in the first unit test; use in‑memory objects.",
      "Mock heavy dependencies so the test drives the design of the `BGate` API."
    ]
  },
  "summary": "The test is fast and repeatable but falls short on readability, maintainability, atomicity, necessity, granularity, and alignment with test‑first TDD practices. Adding clear documentation, decoupling from implementation details, asserting individual metrics, and structuring tests around specifications will substantially improve its quality and resilience."
}

{
  "understandable": {
    "score": 7,
    "rationale": "The test name describes the high‑level rule, but the `rows` literal mixes domain concepts (`predicate`, `anchors`, `counterfactual`, `verifier_report`, `support_level`) with data that are not needed for the behaviour under test. Readers have to decipher which fields actually influence the “no anchor match” outcome.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Extract a helper that builds the minimal viable row for this scenario, exposing only the fields that matter (e.g., `anchors`).",
      "Add a brief comment above the test explaining the domain rule: “The gate must reject a proposal when none of the declared anchors appear in the code snippet.”",
      "If other output fields are required by `compute_b_metrics`, set them to clearly‑named constants like `DEFAULT_UNRELEVANT_OUTPUT` to signal they are filler."
    ]
  },
  "maintainable": {
    "score": 5,
    "rationale": "The test is tightly coupled to the exact shape of `BGateConfig`, `BGateThresholds`, and the internal `metrics` dictionary (\"pass\", \"failures\"). A change such as renaming \"pass\" to \"is_success\" or moving thresholds into a nested config object would cause this test to fail for the wrong reason.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Prefer a domain‑level assertion API provided by the production code, e.g., `assert result.is_successful() is False` and `assert result.failed_due_to_no_anchor_match()`.",
      "Encapsulate config creation in a factory, e.g. `default_gate_config(require_anchor_match=True)`, to shield the test from future field migrations.",
      "Avoid hard‑coded threshold values unless they are part of the specification; rely on default config and only override what the scenario requires."
    ]
  },
  "repeatable": {
    "score": 9,
    "rationale": "The test isolates all external interaction in a temporary directory and uses pure data. There is no reliance on network, database, or nondeterministic timing.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Make the temporary‑file handling explicit in a helper to avoid duplication across tests.",
      "Consider using pytest’s `tmp_path` fixture instead of `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory()` – it integrates better with the test runner and automatically cleans up."
    ]
  },
  "atomic": {
    "score": 8,
    "rationale": "The test focuses on a single rule: the gate must reject when no anchor matches. The two assertions (overall pass flag and presence of the specific reason) are tightly bound to that rule, so a failure instantly points to the missing‑anchor case.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Combine the two assertions into a single domain‑level check to keep the “one assertion per test” principle.",
      "If you keep both asserts, add a comment that the second validates the why of the first, making the intent unmistakable."
    ]
  },
  "necessary": {
    "score": 8,
    "rationale": "The gate’s behavior when no anchors match is a distinct edge case that cannot be inferred from “happy‑path” tests. The test therefore adds unique coverage.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Document the coverage matrix (e.g., in a README or test‑module docstring) showing which scenarios each test addresses.",
      "If the project already has a “missing‑anchor” test, verify that this one adds a different dimension (e.g., thresholds, `require_anchor_match=True`) before keeping both."
    ]
  },
  "granular": {
    "score": 7,
    "rationale": "The test checks only the overall pass flag and the existence of a particular failure reason, which is appropriately narrow. However, the arrangement phase mixes many unrelated fields that could distract from the core assertion.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Separate data preparation from assertion by moving the row definition to a fixture or factory.",
      "If the implementation returns richer failure objects, assert only the type of failure here and let a more focused test probe the richer payload."
    ]
  },
  "fast": {
    "score": 9,
    "rationale": "The test works with a single JSON line, writes to a temporary file, and runs a pure‑Python metric computation. Runtime is negligible.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Avoid the filesystem entirely if `compute_b_metrics` can accept an in‑memory stream (e.g., `io.StringIO`).",
      "If the file‑based API is part of the contract, keep the temporary file but reuse the same file across multiple related tests with a session‑scoped fixture to reduce OS calls."
    ]
  },
  "first_tdd": {
    "score": 6,
    "rationale": "The test follows an arrange‑act‑assert layout, but the “arrange” section is noisy with a large literal payload and config boilerplate, making it harder to see the behaviour the test is specifying. A truly first‑TDD test would surface the specification first and hide the plumbing.",
    "suggestions": [
      "Rename the test to a specification‑style phrase such as `specifies_gate_rejects_when_no_anchor_matches_input`.",
      "Extract the plumbing into helpers so the test body reads almost like prose, e.g., `result.should_be_rejected(because=\"no_anchor_matches_input\")`.",
      "Add a failing assertion before the production code exists (e.g., `assert False, \"gate should reject missing anchors\"`) to document the intent of a test‑first cycle."
    ]
  },
  "summary": "The test demonstrates strong repeatability and speed but suffers from readability, maintainability, and first‑TDD deficiencies. Refactoring by extracting row and config factories, using a domain‑level result wrapper for assertions, simplifying file handling, and adopting specification‑style naming will improve understandability, maintainability, granularity, and overall test quality."
}

